    "description": "Conjecture: Let T be a polynomial or rational map with integer coefficients and degree at least 2, and let a_p(n)=T^n(p) be the orbit starting from a prime p, restricted to those pairs (p,n) for which a_p(n) is nonzero and grows unboundedly. Then, outside an explicit exceptional class consisting of maps semiconjugate to monomials or powering maps, the leading-digit distribution of |a_p(n)| over primes p<=X and times 1<=n<=N converges to Benford's law as X,N->infinity, and the discrepancy is asymptotically controlled by equidistribution of log|T^n(p)| mod 1. Test: For broad families such as T(x)=x^2+1, x^2-2, (x^2+1)/(x+1) on integer points where defined, compute the joint digit frequencies over large prime seeds and iterate depths; confirmation requires convergence to log10(1+1/d) for digits d=1,...,9 together with provable mod-1 equidistribution in at least one non-exceptional family, while systematic persistent non-Benford bias in a generic family refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would connect arithmetic dynamics, prime statistics, and digital phenomena, yielding a new universality principle for nonlinear number-theoretic sequences and suggesting digit-law diagnostics for hidden algebraic structure in dynamical systems.",
